s of dollars per year\").\n\nBut I think in financial contexts, \"continuous rate of change\" or \"continuous yield\" typically refers to the continuously compounded rate, which is ln(FV/PV). This is a unitless number that can be expressed as a percentage.\n\n\nI'll verify the calculation by checking the mathematical interpretation. The continuously compounded rate of 0.045 translates to 4.5% when expressed as a percentage. This aligns with the understanding of continuous rate of change in financial mathematics, representing exponential growth over the one-year period.\n\nThe precise calculation confirms the unitless rate, which can be directly converted to a percentage. I'll document this finding carefully to ensure accuracy in the final answer.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_52923e35aa584dd8adacf71a","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/app/answer.txt","content":"0.045"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
